## 1. Executive Summary

**Manager,**

Apparently, the assignment was to build a functioning IT department inside a residential network. Simple enough, right? Except that the infrastructure decided to demonstrate why IT professionals have trust issues.

The objective was to establish a Windows Server Active Directory environment, integrate a physical Kali Linux workstation, enable centralized domain authentication, and maintain domain controller availability independently of an interactive Linux desktop session.

We successfully accomplished those objectives.

The environment now supports:

- Windows Server 2025 Active Directory Domain Services.
- DNS-based domain controller discovery.
- Kerberos authentication and Samba Winbind integration.
- Graphical Linux login using an Active Directory account.
- Domain-based identity and security group resolution.
- A headless VirtualBox domain controller managed through systemd.
- Domain controller persistence after the original Linux user logs out.
- Configured automatic service startup.

The most significant incident involved domain authentication failing at the graphical login screen despite successful command-line authentication.

Root-cause investigation identified a domain controller availability dependency associated with the original graphical VirtualBox session. Migrating the VM to a systemd-managed headless process resolved the observed login failure.

**Current assessment:** Core infrastructure and cross-platform authentication are operational. Backup validation, controlled reboot testing, and production-grade recovery procedures remain outstanding.

In other words, the system works. Now we make sure it keeps working when the universe decides to be inconvenient.

